
window.ROCK_REVIEW = {"prompts":[{"label":"1","title":"In-depth musical analysis + Assignment 1","source":"COURSE SLIDES + YOUR ASSIGNMENT 1","prompt":"Discuss the importance of an in-depth approach to musical analysis. Be prepared to share your findings from Assignment 1.","answer":"<p><strong>Core argument:</strong> an in-depth analysis combines what is happening inside the sound with the social and historical world around it. Use musical evidence such as rhythm, melody, timbre, texture, dynamics, instrumentation, form, solos, improvisation, and lyrics, then connect those details to audience, technology, race, place, industry, politics, and memory.</p>\n    <p><strong>Your Assignment 1 example:</strong> King Crimson's \"21st Century Schizoid Man.\" You described the massive opening guitar riff as almost alarm-like, Greg Lake's distorted voice as inhuman, rapid drums and aggressive saxophone as a collision of heavy rock and jazz, and the whole track as turning Vietnam-era war, Cold War fear, protest, greed, and political corruption into sound. The cover image and later Kanye West \"Power\" sample show that visual image and later reception can also be part of the analysis.</p>\n    <p><strong>Backup comparison:</strong> \"Epitaph\" uses low timpani, rising Mellotron, large orchestral swells, dark lyrics, and a grave-related title to create hopelessness, failed leadership, and Cold War destruction. Your phrase \"a funeral for a world that has not died yet\" is an excellent one-line interpretation.</p>","must":["Define musical and extramusical evidence","Use specific sonic details, not vague opinions","Connect those details to cultural context","Use your King Crimson Assignment 1 as evidence"]},{"label":"2","title":"Old Culture vs. New Culture","source":"COURSE SLIDES","prompt":"Describe the conflict between Old and New Culture during the 1920s. Give examples. How did music reflect the conflict?","answer":"<p><strong>Old Culture:</strong> production, character, religion, an idealized past, local culture, substance. <strong>New Culture:</strong> consumption, personality, science, looking to the future, mass culture, image.</p>\n    <p>The class places jazz on the New Culture side: spontaneous, accessible, interactive, and tied to a new age. \"Cultured music\" represents tradition: learned, exclusive, and with clear divisions between audience and performer. The hostile 1918 press reaction to jazz shows that the controversy was not only about notes, it was about modernity, youth, behavior, and social order.</p>\n    <p><strong>Modern comparison:</strong> the same structure can appear when older institutions criticize youth-driven music, social-media music cultures, new forms of performance, or image-first celebrity. The safest answer is to compare the conflict pattern, not claim the eras are identical.</p>","must":["Memorize the Old/New table","Jazz = spontaneous, accessible, interactive","Cultured music = tradition, learned, exclusive","Explain why a musical conflict becomes a generational conflict"]},{"label":"3","title":"Original Dixieland Jazz Band","source":"REVIEW SHEET + VERIFIED BACKGROUND","prompt":"The Original Dixieland Jazz Band and the first recording of jazz. Discuss its importance and impact on the birth of a pop music industry, technologically and socially.","answer":"<p>The Original Dixieland Jass Band recorded \"Livery Stable Blues\" and \"Dixie Jass Band One-Step\" for Victor in 1917. The release is widely treated as the first commercially issued jazz record and became a major hit.</p>\n    <p><strong>Technological importance:</strong> recording detached a performance from one room and one moment. A local performance style could now be reproduced, bought, replayed, copied, and circulated nationally. The mechanical recording process also shaped the captured sound through horn placement and fidelity limits.</p>\n    <p><strong>Social importance:</strong> the record helped turn jazz into mass culture and a commercial category. It also exposes a major course theme: a white group became the first to record and profit nationally from an African American-derived musical tradition. The record therefore sits at the intersection of technology, race, genre, youth culture, and commodification.</p>","must":["1917","Victor","Livery Stable Blues","Recording turns a local style into reproducible mass culture","Race and credit problem"]},{"label":"4","title":"Phonograph, radio, television, and the soundscape","source":"COURSE SLIDES","prompt":"How did new technologies alter the American soundscape? What effect did each advancement have on sound, industry, and consumers?","answer":"<p><strong>Phonograph:</strong> before recording, hearing music usually required a live performance. Edison appears in the slides in 1877, and Berliner's disc technology helped standardize commercial records. Early fidelity was poor, so ensembles changed instrumentation and placement. Two-to-three-minute sides pushed musicians toward shorter recorded forms. Recording also rewarded repeatable \"clean takes.\" Consumers could replay the same performance, while labels could sell music as a reproducible commodity.</p>\n    <p><strong>Radio:</strong> Marconi's wireless work leads into broadcasting. Radio sends the same sound to huge audiences at once. WLS and WSM barn-dance programs turned regional country music into national culture, and powerful clear-channel stations later carried R&B far beyond local Black markets. Radio changed who could hear what.</p>\n    <p><strong>Television:</strong> television added the performer's body and image to the sound. Elvis's 1956 appearances made movement, sexuality, fashion, and youth behavior visible to a mass national audience, intensifying both celebrity and backlash.</p>\n    <p><strong>Later technologies in the slides:</strong> microphone, electric guitar, transistor radio, LP, 45, stereo, vinyl, and jukebox each changed sound, portability, format, consumer choice, or circulation. Today the same framework applies to streaming, algorithms, short-form video, and home production: ask what changes in sound, distribution, industry power, and listener behavior.</p>","must":["Phonograph = repeatability + sound constraints + shorter sides","Radio = simultaneous mass distribution + regional sounds become national","Television = sound plus image/performance","Use sound, industry, consumer framework"]},{"label":"5","title":"\"The blues are the roots...\"","source":"COURSE SLIDES","prompt":"Discuss the musical and thematic influences of the blues on the development of rock and roll and country.","answer":"<p><strong>Musical roots:</strong> 12-bar blues, AAB lyric form, call and response, vocal inflection, guitar-centered performance, rhythmic drive, improvisation, and expressive interaction between voice and instrument.</p>\n    <p><strong>Thematic roots:</strong> personal experience, work, movement, relationships, suffering, humor, coping, social commentary, and a strong sense of place.</p>\n    <p><strong>Rock route:</strong> country/Delta blues migrates north, becomes amplified Chicago blues, feeds postwar R&B, and then appears directly in Chuck Berry, Little Richard, Bo Diddley, Elvis, and later British rock.</p>\n    <p><strong>Country route:</strong> the slides make the overlap explicit. Jimmie Rodgers' \"Blue Yodel #1\" uses 12-bar blues, AAB, thematic similarities, and falsetto. Lesley Riddle helped A.P. Carter collect/transcribe songs and influenced Mother Maybelle's guitar style. Early country and blues were much more entangled than race/hillbilly marketing categories suggest.</p>","must":["12-bar + AAB","Voice/instrument interplay and call-response","Delta -> Chicago -> R&B -> rock","Jimmie Rodgers and Lesley Riddle show country/blues overlap"]},{"label":"6","title":"Delta Blues","source":"COURSE SLIDES","prompt":"Discuss characteristics and representative artists of Delta Blues. Why is the Delta associated with blues? How did it influence rock and roll?","answer":"<p><strong>Country/Delta sound:</strong> highly improvised, emphasis on the individual, usually one accompanying instrument, guitar increasingly standard, singing styles drawing from field holler/work song/spiritual traditions, and recurring sense of place.</p>\n    <p><strong>Representative figures from the material:</strong> Charley Patton, Son House, Robert Johnson, Bukka White, and later Muddy Waters as the migration bridge. Patton's material is used as a sonic document of history and experience.</p>\n    <p><strong>Why the Delta:</strong> the slides give three answers: success and widespread influence, connection among music/people/landscape, and the blues myth reinforced by media and tourism. Boll-weevil migration into the Delta also belongs in the place/history story.</p>\n    <p><strong>Rock influence:</strong> Delta singing, guitar, form, and expressive practices move with musicians such as Muddy Waters to Chicago, become electric and band-based, then help generate R&B and rock.</p>","must":["Highly improvised individual guitar-centered sound","Patton / Son House / Johnson / Muddy Waters","Three Why-the-Delta reasons","Migration into Chicago is the bridge to rock"]},{"label":"7","title":"Race Records and Hillbilly Records","source":"COURSE SLIDES","prompt":"Why did these divisions emerge? How accurate were they? How have industry divisions shaped contemporary music?","answer":"<p>The categories emerged as record companies learned to target newly visible consumer markets. Mamie Smith's \"Crazy Blues\" sold roughly one million copies in six months in the course notes and helped create blues as a commercial category. Ralph Peer and Fiddlin' John Carson helped establish the hillbilly market for rural white audiences.</p>\n    <p>The divisions were commercially useful but musically inaccurate. Charley Patton's repertoire crossed Black and white country traditions. Jimmie Rodgers used 12-bar blues and AAB. Lesley Riddle helped shape Carter Family repertory and guitar style. Ruth Brown remembered hearing country and western before R&B. The actual sound world was porous even when the labels were racialized.</p>\n    <p>For contemporary comparison, argue that industry categories still organize marketing, playlists, charts, radio formats, audience identity, and expectations, even when artists move across them.</p>","must":["Marketing segmentation, not natural musical walls","Crazy Blues and Ralph Peer/Fiddlin John Carson","Give at least two crossover examples","Connect categories to industry power"]},{"label":"8","title":"Robert Johnson, myth, and authenticity","source":"COURSE SLIDES + ASSIGNED READING","prompt":"Discuss the myth and iconic status of Robert Johnson. Are myths or concepts of authenticity still used to market artists/genres?","answer":"<p>Johnson recorded only 29 sides in 1936-37, and \"Terraplane Blues\" sold about 5,000 copies. His later iconic status therefore cannot be explained by 1930s mass fame alone. The slides explicitly stress that much of the mythology grows in the 1960s and after.</p>\n    <p>The crossroads story, mysterious death stories, biographies seeking evidence that reinforces the legend, John Hammond's advocacy, the 1961 Columbia collection, Eric Clapton and later rock musicians, the 1991 complete recordings, documentaries, tourism, and crossroads sites all helped construct the remembered Johnson.</p>\n    <p>The collective-memory reading gives the conceptual answer: cultural memory is built socially through stories, media, repetition, audiences, and institutions. \"Authenticity\" can still be marketed through origin stories, rawness, place, vintage technology, outsider identity, or claims that an artist is more \"real\" than commercial competitors.</p>","must":["29 recordings, 1936-37","1960s mythology matters more than lifetime stardom","Crossroads + John Hammond + later rock reception","Collective memory is constructed"]},{"label":"9","title":"Chicago blues","source":"COURSE SLIDES","prompt":"Describe Chicago blues styles. What previous styles were borrowed? How was the music altered? Is there a distinct Chicago sound? Why was it key to rock?","answer":"<p><strong>Prewar acoustic Chicago:</strong> Southern migrants brought country/Delta blues into the city. The \"Bluebird Beat\" mixed classic/vaudeville blues and Delta country blues, added jazz influence, bass and drums, and a more defined rhythm for an urban Black audience. Big Bill Broonzy, Tampa Red, Washboard Sam, Robert Nighthawk, and others belong here.</p>\n    <p><strong>Postwar electric Chicago:</strong> amplification becomes decisive. Muddy Waters moved to Chicago in 1943, shifted from acoustic to electric guitar in 1944, and by the late 1940s expanded the group with second guitar, piano, bass, harmonica, and drums. His style kept traditional blues form, bottleneck/slide influence, defined rhythm, Delta-influenced singing, and call-and-response between voice and guitar.</p>\n    <p><strong>Distinct sound:</strong> amplified guitar and harmonica leads, full rhythm section, strong groove, urban volume, and Delta-derived vocal/guitar language. Chess and Vee-Jay plus DJs such as Al Benson and Big Bill Hill helped spread it nationally.</p>\n    <p><strong>Rock connection:</strong> the electric riff, amplified guitar vocabulary, backbeat/rhythm section, and Chess artist network feed directly into Chuck Berry and later British blues-rock.</p>","must":["Bluebird Beat prewar","Muddy Waters postwar electrification","Full rhythm section + amplified guitar/harmonica","Chess/Vee-Jay and national spread","Direct bridge to rock"]},{"label":"10","title":"Define R&B and explain the R&B to rock transition","source":"COURSE SLIDES + WEEK 3 MATERIAL","prompt":"Define and describe rhythm and blues. How and why did the genre develop? How influential was it on rock and roll? How did Ruth Brown, Arthur Crudup, Little Richard, and Chuck Berry discuss the relationship?","answer":"<p>The Rock Reaction slides describe late-1940s R&B as upbeat Black popular music suited for dancing, drawing from <strong>jump blues, boogie-woogie, jazz, Chicago blues, and gospel</strong>. By 1951 the slides frame rock and roll as a specific genre label emerging from that musical world.</p>\n    <p><strong>Why it develops:</strong> urban migration, independent labels, late-night radio, jukeboxes, new recording formats, electric instruments, regional scenes, and youth consumers create new markets and new mixtures.</p>\n    <p><strong>Ruth Brown:</strong> she later said radio helped R&B turn into rock and roll because young listeners could turn the dial and hear music without seeing the performer's race. Music became a \"common denominator\" even in segregated spaces.</p>\n    <p><strong>Arthur Crudup:</strong> Elvis covered Crudup's \"That's All Right.\" Crudup later described Elvis's version as a kind of hillbilly treatment that broadened its appeal. This is a perfect example of blues material being recombined and re-marketed.</p>\n    <p><strong>Little Richard:</strong> the assigned film gives the cleanest one-line formulation: R&B had a baby and people named it rock and roll. His own music combines gospel intensity, boogie/R&B piano, sexual energy, youth appeal, and radical performance image.</p>\n    <p><strong>Chuck Berry:</strong> \"Maybellene\" began from the country song \"Ida Red\"; Chess changed the title and Berry reshaped the lyrics toward youth interests. The result joins country and western with R&B drive and electric guitar.</p>","must":["Late-40s dance R&B = jump/boogie/jazz/Chicago blues/gospel","Urban migration + radio + jukeboxes + labels + tech","Ruth Brown radio common denominator","Crudup -> Elvis","Little Richard R&B-to-rock statement","Berry Ida Red -> Maybellene"]},{"label":"13","title":"R&B artist fingerprints and regional styles","source":"COURSE SLIDES","prompt":"Know musical characteristics of Louis Jordan, Big Joe Turner, Ray Charles, T-Bone Walker, Fats Domino, etc. Compare them and explain regional influence.","answer":"<p><strong>Kansas City / jump / boogie:</strong> Louis Jordan uses small-combo jump blues, riffs, humor, and a danceable beat. Big Joe Turner represents blues shouting, often over Pete Johnson-style boogie-woogie piano.</p>\n    <p><strong>New Orleans:</strong> Fats Domino and Professor Longhair represent rolling piano rhythm and regional groove. Little Richard takes pounding piano, boogie/R&B energy, gospel-derived vocals, and extreme performance intensity into rock.</p>\n    <p><strong>Electric guitar:</strong> T-Bone Walker is a foundational electric blues guitarist with jazz sophistication and single-note lead language. B.B. King develops vocal-like phrasing and vibrato from this tradition.</p>\n    <p><strong>Gospel/R&B:</strong> Ray Charles brings gospel-derived vocal phrasing and call-and-response into secular R&B.</p>\n    <p><strong>Similarity:</strong> all intensify rhythm, danceability, amplification, vocal personality, or instrumental riffs. <strong>Difference:</strong> each region supplies a different accent: Kansas City jump/shouting, New Orleans piano groove, gospel church language, and electric guitar blues.</p>","must":["Kansas City = Jordan / Turner / boogie / shouting","New Orleans = Fats / Little Richard / Professor Longhair","T-Bone = electric guitar","Ray Charles = gospel language","Compare, do not just list"]},{"label":"14","title":"B.B. King's guitar style and influences","source":"COURSE SLIDES + KING INTERVIEWS","prompt":"Discuss B.B. King's guitar style and its influence. What artists did King cite as influential?","answer":"<p>The slide gives the essential technique: <strong>right hand = precise, complex, vocal-like phrasing; left hand = vibrato</strong>. King explained that when he stopped singing orally, he began to \"sing\" through Lucille. The guitar therefore acts almost like a second voice rather than continuous rhythm accompaniment.</p>\n    <p>King repeatedly cited <strong>T-Bone Walker</strong> as a transformative electric-guitar influence. In interviews he also named <strong>Lonnie Johnson, Blind Lemon Jefferson, Charlie Christian, and Django Reinhardt</strong>, with Bukka White and other Delta musicians part of his early sound world.</p>\n    <p>His influence on later blues and rock lies in economical single-note lead playing, expressive bends, vibrato, vocal phrasing, and making electric-guitar tone an emotional focal point.</p>","must":["Right hand vocal-like phrasing","Left hand vibrato","Lucille as second voice","T-Bone Walker first","Know Lonnie Johnson, Blind Lemon Jefferson, Charlie Christian, Django Reinhardt"]},{"label":"15","title":"Independent labels","source":"COURSE SLIDES","prompt":"Explain the importance of independent labels for R&B and rock and roll. Know the labels and artists discussed.","answer":"<p>Independent labels could target local Black audiences and regional sounds that large labels often ignored, move quickly, work with radio and jukebox markets, and turn local styles into national records.</p>\n    <p><strong>Exact slide map:</strong> Sun, Memphis: B.B. King, Howlin' Wolf, Junior Parker. Chess, Chicago: Muddy Waters, Little Milton, Buddy Guy, Chuck Berry, Willie Dixon. Atlantic, New York: Ray Charles, Ruth Brown, Big Joe Turner. Imperial, Los Angeles: T-Bone Walker, Fats Domino. Specialty, Los Angeles: Lloyd Price, Little Richard. King, Cincinnati: the Dominoes, the Platters, Wynonie Harris, James Brown.</p>\n    <p>The labels are important because \"rock and roll\" does not appear from a single major-company invention. It grows from many regional R&B markets connected through recording, DJs, jukeboxes, touring, and crossover.</p>","must":["Memorize label-city-artist map","Explain why independents could take risks","Connect local scenes to national crossover"]},{"label":"16A","title":"Willie Dixon and the Chess Brothers","source":"COURSE SLIDES","prompt":"Explain Willie Dixon and the Chess Brothers. Know representative artists and styles from class/readings.","answer":"<p>Chess was established in 1949 by <strong>Leonard and Phil Chess</strong>. The label became a central institution in electric Chicago blues and later early rock.</p>\n    <p><strong>Willie Dixon</strong> functioned as producer, talent scout, songwriter, bassist, arranger, and repertoire builder. The slides credit him with more than 200 songs and specifically list \"Hoochie Coochie Man,\" \"Little Red Rooster,\" and \"Back Door Man.\"</p>\n    <p>Representative Chess-associated figures in the slides include Muddy Waters, Howlin' Wolf, Sonny Boy Williamson, Buddy Guy, Robert Nighthawk, Little Walter, Willie Dixon, John Lee Hooker, Elmore James, and later Chuck Berry. The key argument is that Chess turned an urban Black regional sound into a recording catalog that became foundational to rock, especially British rock.</p>","must":["Leonard + Phil Chess, 1949","Dixon = producer/talent scout/songwriter","200+ songs and three examples","Muddy/Howlin Wolf/Little Walter/Buddy Guy/Chuck Berry","Chess connects Chicago blues to rock"]},{"label":"16B","title":"Chuck Berry, Ruth Brown, Little Richard, Bo Diddley","source":"COURSE REVIEW + WEEK 3 MATERIAL","prompt":"Discuss their music. How did it bridge R&B and rock and roll?","answer":"<p><strong>Chuck Berry:</strong> electric guitar riffs, R&B rhythm, country source material, and lyrics recentered on teenagers, cars, school, dancing, and romance. \"Ida Red\" becomes \"Maybellene.\"</p>\n    <p><strong>Ruth Brown:</strong> forceful R&B singing, rhythmic directness, Atlantic's early success, and crossover touring. Her own account stresses radio breaking down the visual racial barrier for young listeners.</p>\n    <p><strong>Little Richard:</strong> pounding piano, gospel-derived shouting, boogie/R&B drive, sexualized energy, flamboyant image, and a performance style that challenged racial and gender expectations. He is one of the clearest cases of R&B becoming what the market calls rock.</p>\n    <p><strong>Bo Diddley:</strong> a distinctive syncopated beat, repeated guitar/rhythm patterns, percussion, and a powerful stage persona. His innovation makes rhythm itself a hook later rock musicians could borrow.</p>\n    <p>Together they show that rock is not a clean break from R&B. The sound, performers, circuits, and audiences overlap while marketing labels shift.</p>","must":["One sonic fingerprint per artist","One social/industry point per artist","End with continuity, not clean break"]},{"label":"17","title":"Sam Phillips and Sun Records","source":"COURSE SLIDES + SAM PHILLIPS BACKGROUND","prompt":"What inspired Phillips to open the Memphis Recording Service? Who were the first artists to record on Sun?","answer":"<p>Phillips came from radio and was drawn to spontaneous, individual sound. His stated purpose in opening Memphis Recording Service in 1950 was to record great Black Southern artists who had few commercial outlets. The studio motto in the slides is <strong>\"We Record Anything, Anywhere, Anytime.\"</strong></p>\n    <p>The slide deck says the studio initially recorded Delta/blues musicians and leased recordings to the Chess brothers. It lists <strong>Rufus Thomas, James Cotton, B.B. King, Little Milton, Howlin' Wolf, Frank Frost, Ike Turner, and Roscoe Gordon</strong>. Another course slide summarizes early Sun with B.B. King, Howlin' Wolf, and Junior Parker.</p>\n    <p>Then the same studio becomes the rockabilly laboratory: Elvis combines Arthur Crudup's R&B/blues \"That's All Right\" with Bill Monroe's country/bluegrass \"Blue Moon of Kentucky\" in 1954.</p>","must":["Memphis Recording Service 1950","Radio background + desire to record Black Southern artists","Motto","Know the slide list of early artists","Elvis later combines Crudup + Bill Monroe"]},{"label":"18","title":"Rock and roll and the sociopolitical 1950s","source":"COURSE SLIDES","prompt":"How did rock shape and reflect the sociopolitical climate of the 1950s? How did America respond? Why was it controversial?","answer":"<p>The slides place rock next to major civil-rights events: Jackie Robinson, Brown v. Board, the Montgomery Bus Boycott, and Little Rock. Rock is not the cause of those events, but it belongs to the same era of contested racial boundaries and changing American identity.</p>\n    <p><strong>Youth culture:</strong> new modes of dissemination made music, fashion, and film increasingly youth-directed. Transistor radio, 45s, jukeboxes, radio DJs, and television gave young listeners more autonomy.</p>\n    <p><strong>Why controversial:</strong> R&B and rock crossed racial markets, encouraged dancing and mixed audiences, centered teenage identity, and made sexuality and physical performance visible. Elvis's television appearances turned movement into a national visual controversy.</p>\n    <p><strong>Responses:</strong> cover records offered \"cleaned up\" versions, Pat Boone described making lyrics more \"vanilla,\" labels promoted boy-next-door teen idols, radio stations and record companies censored R&B lyrics, jukeboxes were confiscated, performers were attacked, and payola scrutiny led to government intervention.</p>","must":["Place rock beside Civil Rights era change without claiming simple causation","Youth culture + new tech","Race + sexuality + dancing + autonomy","Covers + teen idols + censorship + payola"]}],"extraCards":[{"id":"fx01","world":"listen","front":"Old Culture","back":"Production, character, religion, idealized past, local culture, substance.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx02","world":"listen","front":"New Culture","back":"Consumption, personality, science, future, mass culture, image.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx03","world":"listen","front":"Jazz vs cultured music","back":"Jazz: new age, spontaneous, accessible, interactive. Cultured music: tradition, learned, exclusive, clear audience/performer divisions.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx04","world":"tech","front":"ODJB 1917","back":"Original Dixieland Jass Band, Victor, Livery Stable Blues / Dixie Jass Band One-Step, first widely issued jazz record.","source":"REVIEW + VERIFIED BACKGROUND"},{"id":"fx05","world":"tech","front":"Edison phonograph date","back":"1877.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx06","world":"tech","front":"Why early records were short","back":"Berliner-era discs held roughly 2-3 minutes, so composers and performers edited music to fit the medium.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx07","world":"tech","front":"Clean-take effect","back":"Recording made tiny errors repeatable forever, so studio consistency became a new kind of performance skill.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx08","world":"tech","front":"Marconi radio dates","back":"1896 patent, 1901 transatlantic signal.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx09","world":"tech","front":"WSM to Grand Ole Opry","back":"WSM Barn Dance starts 1925, becomes the Grand Ole Opry in 1927, later 50,000-watt clear-channel reach helps make Nashville a country center.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx10","world":"tech","front":"WLAC R&B","back":"By 1946, late-night R&B on a 50,000-watt clear-channel Nashville station helped spread Black music across regions.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx11","world":"markets","front":"Crazy Blues sales in course notes","back":"About one million copies in six months.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx12","world":"markets","front":"Bristol Sessions","back":"1927, called the Big Bang of Country Music in the slides, associated with Jimmie Rodgers and the Carter Family.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx13","world":"markets","front":"Lesley Riddle","back":"Helped A.P. Carter collect/transcribe songs and influenced Mother Maybelle's guitar style.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx14","world":"markets","front":"Carter Scratch","back":"Melody on bass strings with rhythmic strumming on upper strings.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx15","world":"markets","front":"Jimmie Rodgers Blue Yodel #1","back":"1927, 12-bar blues, AAB rhyme scheme, thematic similarities, falsetto singing.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx16","world":"blues","front":"Charley Patton as sonic document","back":"The slides use Mississippi Boll Weevil Blues to connect music to history, experience, coping, communication, and social commentary.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx17","world":"rnb","front":"Bluebird Beat","back":"Prewar Chicago mixture of classic and country blues with jazz influence, bass/drums, and a more defined rhythm.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx18","world":"rnb","front":"Big Bill Broonzy","back":"Called Father of the Chicago Blues in the slides; House Rent Stomp (1927).","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx19","world":"rnb","front":"Tampa Red","back":"Guitar Wizard, slide guitarist, backed Ma Rainey, part of the Hokum Boys.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx20","world":"rnb","front":"Muddy Waters Chicago timeline","back":"1941 recorded with Lomax, 1943 moved to Chicago, 1944 acoustic to electric, late 1940s expanded group.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx21","world":"rnb","front":"Muddy Waters sound","back":"Bottleneck/slide, traditional blues form, defined rhythm, voice/guitar call-response, electric guitar, Delta-influenced singing.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx22","world":"rnb","front":"Chess facts","back":"Established 1949 by Leonard and Phil Chess. Willie Dixon: producer/talent scout/songwriter, 200+ songs.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx23","world":"rnb","front":"B.B. King technique","back":"Right hand: precise, complex, vocal-like phrasing. Left hand: vibrato.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx24","world":"rnb","front":"B.B. King major influences","back":"T-Bone Walker, Lonnie Johnson, Blind Lemon Jefferson, Charlie Christian, Django Reinhardt.","source":"KING INTERVIEWS"},{"id":"fx25","world":"bridge","front":"R&B ingredients in Rock Reaction slides","back":"Jump, boogie-woogie, jazz, Chicago blues, gospel.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx26","world":"bridge","front":"Chuck Berry Maybellene origin","back":"Country song Ida Red, renamed and lyrically reshaped toward youth interests, mixing R&B with country/western.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx27","world":"bridge","front":"Elvis first Sun single sides","back":"That's All Right, Arthur Crudup cover, and Blue Moon of Kentucky, Bill Monroe cover.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx28","world":"reaction","front":"1950s rock technologies","back":"Microphone 1926, electric guitar 1932, transistor radio 1947, LP 1947/48, 45 1949, stereo 1957.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx29","world":"reaction","front":"Jukebox significance","back":"Bypassed radio, played banned songs, let kids choose music, created social listening spaces, expanded selection, made money for the industry.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx30","world":"reaction","front":"Independent label map","back":"Sun-Memphis; Chess-Chicago; Atlantic-New York; Imperial-Los Angeles; Specialty-Los Angeles; King-Cincinnati.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx31","world":"reaction","front":"Pat Boone cover logic","back":"A cleaned-up R&B alternative for mainstream pop markets. Boone said he changed lyrics and made them more vanilla.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx32","world":"reaction","front":"Teen idols","back":"Fabian, Frankie Avalon, Neil Sedaka, Ricky Nelson. Boy-next-door alternative following cover-record success.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx33","world":"revival","front":"Folk-revival rediscoveries","back":"Mississippi John Hurt, Skip James, Gary Davis, Son House, Bukka/Booker White, Doc Watson, Bill Monroe.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx34","world":"revival","front":"How revival marketed tradition","back":"Periodicals such as Shout and Broadside, Hootenanny TV, Vanguard/Folkways reissues, books by Samuel Charters and Paul Oliver.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx35","world":"revival","front":"Woody Guthrie","back":"Dust Bowl Ballads, social storytelling, and the idea that folk should comfort the disturbed and disturb the comfortable.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx36","world":"revival","front":"Dylan early repertoire","back":"His first album mixed originals with traditional songs and material linked to older folk/blues performers, making him part of a living tradition before the major protest-song breakthrough.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx37","world":"studio","front":"Phil Spector","back":"Established girl-group sound, controlled the studio, doubled instruments, described Wall of Sound as little symphonies for kids.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx38","world":"studio","front":"Motown slide fact","back":"Berry Gordy, Tamla, Shop Around by Smokey Robinson reached #2 pop.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx39","world":"british","front":"British blues encounters","back":"Big Bill Broonzy 1951, Muddy Waters 1958, American Folk Blues Festival 1962-66.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"fx40","world":"british","front":"Skiffle chain","back":"Lead Belly's Rock Island Line -> Lonnie Donegan -> skiffle youth scene -> Quarry Men and future British rock musicians.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"}],"extraQuestions":[{"id":"qx001","world":"listen","topic":"Old/New Culture","q":"Which cluster belongs to Old Culture in the class slides?","choices":["Consumption, personality, science","Production, character, religion","Mass culture, image, future","Science, personality, local culture"],"a":1,"why":"The slide pairs Old Culture with production, character, religion, idealized past, local culture, and substance.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx002","world":"listen","topic":"Old/New Culture","q":"Which cluster belongs to New Culture?","choices":["Production, religion, local culture","Character, substance, idealized past","Consumption, personality, mass culture","Tradition, exclusivity, fixed roles"],"a":2,"why":"New Culture is associated with consumption, personality, science, future, mass culture, and image.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx003","world":"listen","topic":"Jazz","q":"Which trait is assigned to jazz rather than cultured music in the slide comparison?","choices":["Exclusive","Spontaneous","Clear performer-audience division","Learned tradition"],"a":1,"why":"Jazz is framed as spontaneous, accessible, interactive, and music of a new age.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx004","world":"listen","topic":"Jazz controversy","q":"A 1918 newspaper calling jazz an atrocity with no musical value is strongest evidence for what?","choices":["A purely technical disagreement about harmony","A wider social fear surrounding New Culture music","Universal adult acceptance of jazz","The disappearance of mass culture"],"a":1,"why":"The quote shows cultural anxiety, not just musical criticism.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx005","world":"tech","topic":"ODJB","q":"What year is associated with the Original Dixieland Jass Band's first widely issued jazz recordings?","choices":["1901","1917","1927","1946"],"a":1,"why":"The Victor sides were recorded and issued in 1917.","source":"REVIEW + VERIFIED BACKGROUND"},{"id":"qx006","world":"tech","topic":"ODJB","q":"Why is the ODJB recording socially complicated?","choices":["It was made before records existed","A white band became the first nationally recorded representative of an African American-derived tradition","The group refused to use technology","It was banned from every market"],"a":1,"why":"The first-recording story reveals questions of race, credit, and commodification.","source":"REVIEW + VERIFIED BACKGROUND"},{"id":"qx007","world":"tech","topic":"Phonograph","q":"Thomas Edison is associated with which date in the slides?","choices":["1857","1877","1896","1920"],"a":1,"why":"The slides list Edison and the phonograph at 1877.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx008","world":"tech","topic":"Recording format","q":"What did two-to-three-minute disc capacity encourage?","choices":["Longer symphonies on one side","Editing and shorter recorded forms","No vocals","The end of popular songs"],"a":1,"why":"Musicians and composers altered pieces to fit the medium.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx009","world":"tech","topic":"Studio performance","q":"Why did recording reward a clean take?","choices":["Mistakes became permanently repeatable","Audiences could fix mistakes live","Only one instrument could be recorded","Microphones automatically removed errors"],"a":0,"why":"A recorded error could be replayed, so consistency became newly important.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx010","world":"tech","topic":"Radio","q":"Which pair matches the radio timeline in the slides?","choices":["Marconi 1896 patent / 1901 transatlantic signal","Edison 1896 patent / 1901 LP","Berliner 1933 / Marconi 1938","WSM 1901 / NBC 1896"],"a":0,"why":"Those are the Marconi milestones shown.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx011","world":"tech","topic":"Country radio","q":"What did 50,000-watt clear-channel WSM help do?","choices":["Keep country music confined to Nashville","Broadcast the Opry across more than 30 states and strengthen Nashville's national identity","End the Barn Dance","Eliminate rural listeners"],"a":1,"why":"The slide explicitly links high power and national dissemination to Nashville's country-music status.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx012","world":"tech","topic":"R&B radio","q":"What is the best significance of late-night clear-channel R&B broadcasts such as WLAC?","choices":["They made R&B less geographically mobile","They allowed Black popular music to reach listeners across large regions","They replaced records completely","They only served local daytime listeners"],"a":1,"why":"High-power night broadcasting could cross normal market boundaries.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx013","world":"markets","topic":"Crazy Blues","q":"According to the course notes, roughly how quickly did Crazy Blues sell one million copies?","choices":["One week","Six months","Five years","Twenty years"],"a":1,"why":"The notes call it a monster hit, one million in six months.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx014","world":"markets","topic":"Bristol Sessions","q":"The slides call the 1927 Bristol Sessions what?","choices":["The first Wall of Sound","The Big Bang of Country Music","The Chicago migration","The first rock concert"],"a":1,"why":"That exact phrase appears in the deck.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx015","world":"markets","topic":"Country-blues overlap","q":"Which Jimmie Rodgers feature most directly demonstrates blues influence?","choices":["Twelve-bar blues and AAB rhyme scheme","Wall of Sound layering","Bo Diddley beat","Motown tambourine"],"a":0,"why":"Blue Yodel #1 is explicitly marked 12-bar, AAB, thematic similarities, and falsetto.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx016","world":"markets","topic":"Lesley Riddle","q":"What did Lesley Riddle contribute to the Carter Family story?","choices":["He owned Atlantic Records","He helped A.P. Carter collect/transcribe songs and influenced Maybelle's guitar style","He invented the phonograph","He produced Phil Spector"],"a":1,"why":"The slides give both roles.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx017","world":"markets","topic":"Carter Scratch","q":"What is the Carter Scratch?","choices":["A drum shuffle","Melody on bass strings with rhythmic strumming on the top strings","A vocal scream","A radio format"],"a":1,"why":"That is the technique described in the slides.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx018","world":"blues","topic":"Charley Patton","q":"Why is Mississippi Boll Weevil Blues useful to the course?","choices":["It proves blues avoided real events","It works as a musical document connecting history, experience, coping, communication, and social commentary","It is a Motown song","It has no relationship to migration"],"a":1,"why":"The course explicitly calls the blues a sonic snapshot/document.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx019","world":"blues","topic":"Boll weevil","q":"What migration fact appears in the blues slides?","choices":["The boll weevil attracted sharecroppers away from the Delta","Boll-weevil infestation pushed many sharecroppers toward the Delta, where winter flooding reduced weevil survival","The Delta had no cotton","Sharecroppers moved only to Chicago before 1900"],"a":1,"why":"The slides connect ecology, migration, labor, and the Delta.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx020","world":"rnb","topic":"Chicago blues","q":"What was the Bluebird Beat?","choices":["A Motown dance","A sweetened prewar Chicago blend of classic and country blues with jazz influence, bass/drums, and more defined rhythm","A British skiffle beat","A 1960s surf rhythm"],"a":1,"why":"That description comes directly from the Chicago slides.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx021","world":"rnb","topic":"Chicago artists","q":"Who is called the Father of the Chicago Blues in the slides?","choices":["Big Bill Broonzy","T-Bone Walker","Ray Charles","Phil Spector"],"a":0,"why":"The slide labels Big Bill Broonzy that way.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx022","world":"rnb","topic":"Chicago artists","q":"Tampa Red is identified as what kind of guitarist?","choices":["Classical guitarist","Guitar Wizard and slide guitarist","Surf guitarist","Motown session player"],"a":1,"why":"That is the slide wording.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx023","world":"rnb","topic":"Muddy Waters","q":"Which sequence is correct?","choices":["1941 Lomax recording, 1943 move to Chicago, 1944 electric guitar, late-40s expanded group","1944 move to Chicago, 1941 electric, 1955 acoustic","1951 London debut, 1958 Delta recording","1936 San Antonio, 1941 Motown"],"a":0,"why":"That exact timeline is in the Chicago blues deck.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx024","world":"rnb","topic":"Muddy Waters","q":"Which is NOT listed as a Muddy Waters musical characteristic?","choices":["Bottleneck style","Call and response between voice and guitar","Well-defined rhythm","Complete rejection of traditional blues form"],"a":3,"why":"The slides explicitly say traditional blues form.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx025","world":"rnb","topic":"Chicago institutions","q":"Which pair of labels is highlighted for the Chicago sound?","choices":["Chess and Vee-Jay","Sun and Specialty","Atlantic and Imperial","Motown and King"],"a":0,"why":"The slide lists Chess and Vee-Jay.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx026","world":"rnb","topic":"Chess","q":"Who established Chess Records in 1949?","choices":["Sam Phillips and Dewey Phillips","Leonard and Phil Chess","Berry Gordy and Smokey Robinson","Jerry Leiber and Mike Stoller"],"a":1,"why":"The deck lists Leonard and Phil Chess.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx027","world":"rnb","topic":"Willie Dixon","q":"Which song is NOT in the slide's Willie Dixon examples?","choices":["Hoochie Coochie Man","Little Red Rooster","Back Door Man","Blowin in the Wind"],"a":3,"why":"The first three are on the Willie Dixon slide.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx028","world":"rnb","topic":"B.B. King","q":"How does the slide split B.B. King's guitar technique?","choices":["Right hand vibrato / left hand rhythm","Right hand precise vocal-like phrasing / left hand vibrato","Right hand slide / left hand percussion","Right hand chords only / left hand muting"],"a":1,"why":"That is the exact slide formulation.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx029","world":"rnb","topic":"B.B. King influences","q":"Which group contains only musicians B.B. King cited as major guitar influences?","choices":["T-Bone Walker, Lonnie Johnson, Blind Lemon Jefferson, Charlie Christian, Django Reinhardt","Phil Spector, Bob Dylan, Pat Boone, Elvis, George Martin","Ruth Brown, Mamie Smith, Aretha Franklin, Wanda Jackson, Madonna","Berry Gordy, Sam Phillips, John Hammond, Ralph Peer, Alan Freed"],"a":0,"why":"King named those blues and jazz guitarists across interviews.","source":"KING INTERVIEWS"},{"id":"qx030","world":"bridge","topic":"R&B definition","q":"The Rock Reaction slides describe late-1940s R&B as what?","choices":["Upbeat music suited for dancing, drawing on jump/boogie/jazz/Chicago blues/gospel","Slow orchestral pop only","A British folk category","A white-only country format"],"a":0,"why":"That exact combination appears at the start of the deck.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx031","world":"bridge","topic":"Chuck Berry","q":"What did the professor's Chuck Berry notes say changed from Ida Red to Maybellene?","choices":["Only the guitar tuning","The name and lyrics, reshaped toward youth interest","Nothing at all","It became an instrumental"],"a":1,"why":"The notes say changed name and lyrics to reflect youth interest, combining R&B with country/western.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx032","world":"bridge","topic":"Elvis","q":"Which pairing correctly identifies Elvis's 1954 Sun covers?","choices":["That's All Right, Arthur Crudup / Blue Moon of Kentucky, Bill Monroe","Maybellene, Muddy Waters / Hoochie Coochie Man, Willie Dixon","Crazy Blues, Mamie Smith / Cross Road Blues, Robert Johnson","Shop Around, Smokey Robinson / Be My Baby, Ronettes"],"a":0,"why":"The slide puts the Black blues source and white country source side by side.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx033","world":"bridge","topic":"Ruth Brown","q":"What did Ruth Brown say helped R&B turn into rock and roll?","choices":["Radio let kids hear music without seeing the performer's race","Television removed dancing","Record companies ended crossover","Country music disappeared"],"a":0,"why":"Her interview emphasizes radio as the intermediary and music as a common denominator.","source":"RUTH BROWN INTERVIEW"},{"id":"qx034","world":"bridge","topic":"Arthur Crudup","q":"How did Crudup describe Elvis's treatment of That's All Right?","choices":["He said Elvis turned it into opera","He said Elvis made it into a kind of hillbilly record, and he liked its broader appeal","He denied writing it","He called it Motown"],"a":1,"why":"Crudup recognized a stylistic transformation that helped the song cross markets.","source":"CRUDUP INTERVIEW"},{"id":"qx035","world":"bridge","topic":"Little Richard","q":"Which statement best captures Little Richard's own formulation in the assigned film?","choices":["Rock and roll was unrelated to R&B","Rhythm and blues had a baby and somebody named it rock and roll","Country had no influence on rock","Rock was invented in Britain"],"a":1,"why":"The film uses this as a compact statement of continuity and renaming.","source":"ASSIGNED FILM"},{"id":"qx036","world":"reaction","topic":"Technology","q":"Which technology-date pairing is wrong according to the slides?","choices":["Microphone, 1926","Electric guitar, 1932","45 rpm, 1949","Stereophonic sound, 1947"],"a":3,"why":"The slide lists stereophonic sound at 1957.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx037","world":"reaction","topic":"Jukebox","q":"Which was NOT a jukebox effect listed in class?","choices":["Bypassed radio","Let kids choose their own music","Brought music to social spaces","Prevented banned songs from being heard"],"a":3,"why":"The slide says jukeboxes could play songs banned from radio.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx038","world":"reaction","topic":"Independent labels","q":"Which label-city-artist pairing matches the professor's slide?","choices":["Atlantic-New York-Ruth Brown","Chess-Los Angeles-Fats Domino","Specialty-Memphis-B.B. King","Sun-Cincinnati-James Brown"],"a":0,"why":"Atlantic is listed in New York with Ray Charles, Ruth Brown, and Big Joe Turner.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx039","world":"reaction","topic":"Independent labels","q":"Which label is paired with Little Richard?","choices":["Specialty","Imperial","Chess","Sun"],"a":0,"why":"Specialty, Los Angeles, is paired with Lloyd Price and Little Richard.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx040","world":"reaction","topic":"Elvis TV","q":"Why did television intensify the Elvis controversy?","choices":["It hid his body","It made physical movement and sexuality visible to a mass audience","It prevented national exposure","It removed youth culture from music"],"a":1,"why":"TV made image and movement part of the national rock experience.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx041","world":"reaction","topic":"Cover records","q":"What was the function of many cover records according to the slides?","choices":["Make R&B rougher","Offer a cleaned-up alternative to R&B","Return music to live-only performance","End youth markets"],"a":1,"why":"The slide literally calls them an alternative to R&B, cleaned-up version of the same song.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx042","world":"reaction","topic":"Pat Boone","q":"What word did Pat Boone use for the effect of changing lyrics in cover records?","choices":["Electric","Vanilla","Delta","Psychedelic"],"a":1,"why":"The quote says the changes made it more vanilla.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx043","world":"reaction","topic":"Backlash","q":"Which sequence belongs to the Attack on rock material?","choices":["Censor lyrics, confiscate jukeboxes, attack performers, government intervention/payola","Promote only Black originals, end censorship, abolish radio","Ban television, close every label, eliminate dancing nationwide","Support R&B lyrics without restriction"],"a":0,"why":"Those are the specific backlash mechanisms listed.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx044","world":"revival","topic":"Folk revival","q":"Which artist is part of the rediscovered/traditional-musician list in the slides?","choices":["Mississippi John Hurt","Phil Spector","Pat Boone","Ruth Brown"],"a":0,"why":"The revival slide lists Hurt, Skip James, Gary Davis, Son House, Booker/Bukka White, and others.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx045","world":"revival","topic":"Folk revival","q":"Which method helped market folk tradition to new audiences?","choices":["Hootenanny television and Folkways/Vanguard reissues","Only jukeboxes","Motown choreography","Chess electric amplification"],"a":0,"why":"Those are specific slide examples of revival infrastructure.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx046","world":"revival","topic":"Woody Guthrie","q":"Which phrase captures the political role assigned to a folk singer in the slides?","choices":["Comfort disturbed people and disturb comfortable people","Make every song three minutes","Never mention politics","Only preserve romance songs"],"a":0,"why":"The Woody Guthrie material gives that line.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx047","world":"revival","topic":"Dylan","q":"Why does Dylan's early first-album track list matter?","choices":["It shows he initially stood inside a larger folk/blues tradition of traditional songs and earlier artists","It proves he wrote every song alone","It was a Wall of Sound record","It contains no blues material"],"a":0,"why":"The track list mixes originals with traditional and older folk/blues material.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx048","world":"revival","topic":"Dylan","q":"Which songs in the slide set show Dylan's shift toward explicit social commentary?","choices":["Blowin in the Wind, Oxford Town, Masters of War","Maybellene, Rocket 88, Bear Cat","Be My Baby, Shop Around, The Fat Man","Cross Road Blues, Crazy Blues, Hoochie Coochie Man"],"a":0,"why":"Those are the Dylan examples in the deck.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx049","world":"studio","topic":"Phil Spector","q":"Which is closest to the professor's Wall of Sound explanation?","choices":["Doubled instruments and total studio control, little symphonies for kids","One guitar and one voice only","No producer involvement","Pure live documentation"],"a":0,"why":"The slide links Spector's control, doubled instruments, and the Wagnerian little-symphonies idea.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx050","world":"studio","topic":"Girl groups","q":"Which group is NOT on the girl-group slide?","choices":["The Ronettes","The Crystals","The Shangri-Las","The Rolling Stones"],"a":3,"why":"The Rolling Stones are not a girl group.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx051","world":"studio","topic":"Motown","q":"Which fact appears on the Motown slide?","choices":["Berry Gordy, Tamla, Shop Around reached #2 pop","Sam Phillips founded Motown","Motown began with Livery Stable Blues","Motown was a Chicago blues label"],"a":0,"why":"That is the slide's core Motown fact cluster.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx052","world":"british","topic":"British blues","q":"Which chronology is correct?","choices":["Big Bill Broonzy 1951, Muddy Waters 1958, American Folk Blues Festival 1962-66","Muddy Waters 1920, Broonzy 1970, festival 1980","Beatles 1917, Stones 1920, Dylan 1930","Motown 1951, Sun 1958, Chess 1962"],"a":0,"why":"Those dates are directly listed in the British blues slides.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx053","world":"british","topic":"Muddy in Britain","q":"How did the London Times characterize Muddy Waters's 1958 sound in the slide?","choices":["Screaming guitar and howling piano","Quiet acoustic folk","Little symphonies for kids","No rhythm section"],"a":0,"why":"That quote signals how startling electric Chicago blues sounded to British listeners.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx054","world":"british","topic":"Skiffle","q":"What chain best captures the skiffle example?","choices":["Lead Belly's Rock Island Line -> Lonnie Donegan -> British youth/skiffle scene","Pat Boone -> Motown -> Delta blues","Phil Spector -> Robert Johnson -> Sun Records","Ruth Brown -> Bristol Sessions -> country radio"],"a":0,"why":"The slide directly pairs Lead Belly and Lonnie Donegan around Rock Island Line.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx055","world":"british","topic":"British rock","q":"Why is skiffle important to later British rock?","choices":["It gave young musicians a simple, roots-based entry point into American folk/blues material and helped feed groups such as the Quarry Men","It eliminated guitars","It was a classical conservatory style","It was created by Motown"],"a":0,"why":"The course sequence moves from skiffle to figures such as Jimmy Page and the Quarry Men.","source":"COURSE SLIDES"},{"id":"qx056","world":"mix","topic":"Synthesis","q":"Which path best demonstrates how technology and race interact in this course?","choices":["Radio lets youth hear Black R&B without seeing the performer's race, helping cross-market listening despite segregation","The phonograph prevents all crossover","Television makes image irrelevant","Jukeboxes reduce listener choice"],"a":0,"why":"Ruth Brown's account makes radio a key bridge across visual racial barriers.","source":"SYNTHESIS"},{"id":"qx057","world":"mix","topic":"Synthesis","q":"Which two examples most directly undermine a strict Black-blues versus white-country divide?","choices":["Jimmie Rodgers using 12-bar/AAB and Lesley Riddle shaping Carter Family repertory","Phil Spector and stereophonic sound","Grand Ole Opry and Elvis TV criticism","Motown and Woodstock"],"a":0,"why":"Both show Black/white musical traditions interacting before rock.","source":"SYNTHESIS"},{"id":"qx058","world":"mix","topic":"Synthesis","q":"What is the strongest overall explanation of early rock and roll?","choices":["A new label and youth market built from already-interacting Black and white musical traditions, amplified by new technology and independent industry networks","A style with no roots before 1955","A purely technological invention","A British import with no American R&B foundation"],"a":0,"why":"That formulation integrates the course's main musical, social, technological, and industry themes.","source":"SYNTHESIS"}],"extraSteps":{"listen":[{"type":"learn","tag":"EXACT SLIDE TABLE","title":"Old Culture versus New Culture","body":"<div class='fingerprint'>\n       <div class='fp'><b>Old Culture</b><span>Production, character, religion, idealized past, local culture, substance.</span></div>\n       <div class='fp'><b>New Culture</b><span>Consumption, personality, science, future, mass culture, image.</span></div>\n     </div><p>The professor then maps jazz onto the New Culture side: music of a new age, spontaneous, accessible, interactive. \"Cultured music\" is paired with tradition, learned behavior, exclusivity, and clear performer/audience divisions.</p>\n     <div class='memory-hook'><span>EXAM MOVE</span><p>Do not just memorize the table. Use jazz to explain why modern sound became a fight about modern values.</p></div>"}],"tech":[{"type":"learn","tag":"FIRST JAZZ RECORD","title":"ODJB: technology creates a national genre moment","body":"<p>The review sheet specifically names the Original Dixieland Jazz Band. Their 1917 Victor release, including \"Livery Stable Blues,\" is widely treated as the first commercially issued jazz record.</p>\n     <p>The important course connection is bigger than a date: mechanical recording turns a local, live, socially contested sound into something that can be reproduced and sold nationally. It also creates a race-and-credit problem because a white band becomes the first recorded national representative of an African American-derived style.</p>"},{"type":"learn","tag":"RADIO BUILDS REGIONS","title":"Country radio and R&B radio do the same thing in different markets","body":"<p>WLS National Barn Dance and WSM Barn Dance show radio turning rural styles into mass culture. WSM becomes the Grand Ole Opry, grows to 50,000-watt clear-channel status, and eventually reaches more than 30 states.</p>\n     <p>Later, powerful late-night stations such as WLAC carry R&B over huge distances. The technology can therefore nationalize a regional style and allow listeners to cross market boundaries from home.</p>"},{"type":"learn","tag":"ROCK-ERA TECH","title":"The format war changes how youth consume music","body":"<div class='fingerprint'>\n       <div class='fp'><b>Microphone</b><span>1926 in the slide timeline.</span></div>\n       <div class='fp'><b>Electric guitar</b><span>1932.</span></div>\n       <div class='fp'><b>Transistor radio</b><span>1947, Regency TR-1 in 1954.</span></div>\n       <div class='fp'><b>LP</b><span>1947/48, album-length listening.</span></div>\n       <div class='fp'><b>45</b><span>1949, portable hit-single format.</span></div>\n       <div class='fp'><b>Stereo</b><span>1957.</span></div>\n     </div><p>Then add the jukebox: it bypassed radio, could play banned songs, let kids choose their own music, brought music into social spaces, expanded selection, and generated reliable industry revenue.</p>"},{"type":"learn","tag":"TELEVISION","title":"Television turns the body into part of the music","body":"<p>Radio can hide race and appearance. Television does the opposite. Elvis's 1956 television appearances made his physical movement visible to tens of millions, and the press response focused on suggestiveness, vulgarity, and taste.</p>\n     <p>That is why television belongs in the technology question: it changes not only distribution but what counts as the musical performance. Image, fashion, movement, sexuality, and audience reaction all become part of the national soundscape.</p>"}],"blues":[{"type":"learn","tag":"SONIC DOCUMENT","title":"Charley Patton turns history into sound","body":"<p>The slides use \"Mississippi Boll Weevil Blues\" to demonstrate the blues as a musical document or sonic snapshot. The song can carry history and lived experience, work as a means of coping, communicate across a community, and function as social commentary.</p>\n     <p>The boll-weevil material also ties ecology to migration: infestation pushed many sharecroppers toward the Delta, while the Delta's winter flooding reduced insect survival. Place is not background decoration, it helps explain the musical community.</p>"}],"markets":[{"type":"learn","tag":"COUNTRY IS NOT SEPARATE","title":"The country side of the roots story","body":"<p>The 1927 Bristol Sessions are called the \"Big Bang of Country Music\" in the slides and introduce Jimmie Rodgers and the Carter Family into the same recording-industry story.</p>\n     <div class='fingerprint'>\n       <div class='fp'><b>Jimmie Rodgers</b><span>\"Blue Yodel #1\": 12-bar blues, AAB rhyme scheme, thematic similarities, falsetto singing.</span></div>\n       <div class='fp'><b>Lesley Riddle</b><span>Helped A.P. Carter collect/transcribe songs and influenced Mother Maybelle's guitar style.</span></div>\n       <div class='fp'><b>Carter Scratch</b><span>Melody on bass strings with rhythmic strumming on upper strings.</span></div>\n       <div class='fp'><b>Point</b><span>The race/hillbilly record categories are much cleaner than the actual music.</span></div>\n     </div>"}],"rnb":[{"type":"learn","tag":"PREWAR CHICAGO","title":"Before electricity, Chicago already had a distinct adaptation","body":"<p>Southern musicians carried country/Delta blues north. The prewar \"Bluebird Beat\" mixed classic blues and country blues, added jazz influence, bass and drums, and a more defined rhythm. It was an urban adaptation, not yet the fully electric postwar sound.</p>\n     <p>Know Big Bill Broonzy, Tampa Red, Washboard Sam, Robert Nighthawk, Roosevelt Sykes, and Sonny Boy Williamson I as part of this scene.</p>"},{"type":"learn","tag":"POSTWAR CHICAGO","title":"Muddy Waters electrifies the migration story","body":"<p>Course timeline: 1941 recorded with Lomax, 1943 moved to Chicago, 1944 shifted from acoustic to electric guitar, late 1940s expanded group with second guitar, piano, bass, harmonica, and drums.</p>\n     <p>His musical fingerprint: bottleneck/slide mastery, traditional blues form, well-defined rhythm, call-and-response between voice and guitar, electric-guitar expression, and a singing style influenced by Delta folk traditions.</p>"},{"type":"learn","tag":"R&B REGIONS","title":"Do not flatten R&B into one sound","body":"<div class='fingerprint'>\n       <div class='fp'><b>Kansas City</b><span>Jump blues, blues shouting, boogie-woogie. Big Joe Turner + Pete Johnson.</span></div>\n       <div class='fp'><b>New Orleans</b><span>Fats Domino, Little Richard, Professor Longhair, piano-centered regional groove.</span></div>\n       <div class='fp'><b>Chicago</b><span>Electric blues, amplified guitar/harmonica, rhythm section, Chess.</span></div>\n       <div class='fp'><b>Gospel/R&B</b><span>Ray Charles brings church-derived phrasing and call-response into secular music.</span></div>\n     </div>"},{"type":"learn","tag":"B.B. KING","title":"The guitar sings when the voice stops","body":"<p>The professor's slide splits King's style cleanly: <strong>right hand, precise and complex vocal-like phrasing; left hand, vibrato</strong>. King says that when he stops singing orally, he begins to sing through Lucille.</p>\n     <p>For influences, know T-Bone Walker first, plus Lonnie Johnson, Blind Lemon Jefferson, Charlie Christian, and Django Reinhardt. The mixture of blues and jazz guitar language helps explain why King's lead style becomes so influential in rock.</p>"},{"type":"learn","tag":"LABEL MAP","title":"Memorize the independent-label network","body":"<div class='fingerprint'>\n       <div class='fp'><b>Sun, Memphis</b><span>B.B. King, Howlin' Wolf, Junior Parker.</span></div>\n       <div class='fp'><b>Chess, Chicago</b><span>Muddy Waters, Little Milton, Buddy Guy, Chuck Berry, Willie Dixon.</span></div>\n       <div class='fp'><b>Atlantic, New York</b><span>Ray Charles, Ruth Brown, Big Joe Turner.</span></div>\n       <div class='fp'><b>Imperial, Los Angeles</b><span>T-Bone Walker, Fats Domino.</span></div>\n       <div class='fp'><b>Specialty, Los Angeles</b><span>Lloyd Price, Little Richard.</span></div>\n       <div class='fp'><b>King, Cincinnati</b><span>The Dominoes, the Platters, Wynonie Harris, James Brown.</span></div>\n     </div>"}],"bridge":[{"type":"learn","tag":"R&B -> ROCK","title":"Use the artists' own stories, not just a textbook label","body":"<p><strong>Ruth Brown:</strong> radio let kids hear performers without seeing race, and she described music becoming a common denominator even in segregated dance spaces.</p>\n     <p><strong>Arthur Crudup:</strong> Elvis turned \"That's All Right\" into a more hillbilly-sounding version that could reach a broader market.</p>\n     <p><strong>Little Richard:</strong> the film gives the memorable formulation that R&B had a baby and it got named rock and roll.</p>\n     <p><strong>Chuck Berry:</strong> starts with the country tune \"Ida Red,\" changes name and lyrics toward youth interest, and combines country/western with R&B.</p>"},{"type":"learn","tag":"SUN BEFORE ELVIS","title":"Sam Phillips records Black Southern artists first","body":"<p>The Sun slide motto is \"We Record Anything, Anywhere, Anytime.\" It says Phillips initially recorded Delta/blues musicians and leased recordings to the Chess brothers.</p>\n     <p>Know the slide list: Rufus Thomas, James Cotton, B.B. King, Little Milton, Howlin' Wolf, Frank Frost, Ike Turner, and Roscoe Gordon. Another label map highlights B.B. King, Howlin' Wolf, and Junior Parker.</p>\n     <div class='memory-hook'><span>WHY IT MATTERS</span><p>Sun's rockabilly story grows out of an earlier Black blues/R&B recording operation.</p></div>"}],"reaction":[{"type":"learn","tag":"YOUTH TECH","title":"Why young listeners suddenly had more control","body":"<p>The transistor radio made listening portable and private. The 45 centered the single. Jukeboxes let listeners choose songs directly and could play records that radio stations banned. Television then gave youth culture a visible national image.</p>\n     <p>That technological autonomy is part of why the music becomes sociopolitically threatening: young people can choose, repeat, dance to, and identify with music adults did not select for them.</p>"},{"type":"learn","tag":"ELVIS AS CASE STUDY","title":"One record combines the whole course","body":"<p>Elvis's 1954 Sun single paired Arthur Crudup's Black blues/R&B \"That's All Right\" with Bill Monroe's white country/bluegrass \"Blue Moon of Kentucky.\" Dewey Phillips played Elvis on \"Red, Hot, and Blue,\" and the slides note 5,000 orders within ten days.</p>\n     <p>Then television magnified the image. The course's press quotations attack his physical movement as suggestive, vulgar, animalistic, and in poor taste. Sound, race, youth, sexuality, image, and technology all collide in one case.</p>"},{"type":"learn","tag":"THE BACKLASH","title":"Cover, replace, censor, investigate","body":"<p><strong>Cover records:</strong> cleaned-up alternatives to R&B. Pat Boone said he changed lyrics and made the material more \"vanilla.\"</p>\n     <p><strong>Teen idols:</strong> boy-next-door alternatives such as Fabian, Frankie Avalon, Neil Sedaka, and Ricky Nelson.</p>\n     <p><strong>Attack:</strong> censorship of R&B lyrics, confiscated jukeboxes, attacks on performers, and government intervention around payola.</p>"}],"revival":[{"type":"learn","tag":"HOW REVIVAL WORKED","title":"Rediscovery was an industry too","body":"<p>The professor lists Mississippi John Hurt, Skip James, Gary Davis, Son House, Booker/Bukka White, Doc Watson, and Bill Monroe among traditional musicians brought to new audiences.</p>\n     <p>Infrastructure mattered: periodicals such as Shout and Broadside, the television program Hootenanny, reissues from Vanguard and Folkways, Samuel Charters's <em>Country Blues</em> (1959), and Paul Oliver's <em>Blues Fell This Morning</em> (1960).</p>\n     <div class='callout'>Connect this back to Robert Johnson. Rediscovery can preserve tradition while also packaging a new version of \"authenticity.\"</div>"},{"type":"learn","tag":"DYLAN IN CONTEXT","title":"Dylan begins inside tradition, then pushes the lyrical frame","body":"<p>The first-album slide is full of traditional material and songs linked to older folk/blues performers. That matters because Dylan is not arriving outside the tradition.</p>\n     <p>Then \"Blowin' in the Wind,\" \"Oxford Town,\" and \"Masters of War\" demonstrate the move toward explicit political and social commentary. The professor's Dylan quote directly rejects the idea that popular songs should only deal with love and sex.</p>"}],"studio":[{"type":"learn","tag":"PHIL SPECTOR","title":"Control the studio, enlarge the record","body":"<p>The slide says Spector established the girl-group sound, took control of everything in the studio, and built the Wall of Sound by doubling instruments. The \"Wagnerian\" comparison and \"little symphonies for kids\" phrase emphasize scale and producer control.</p>\n     <p>Girl-group examples in the slide include the Shirelles, Chiffons, Blossoms, Shangri-Las, Crystals, Ronettes, and Supremes.</p>"},{"type":"learn","tag":"MOTOWN","title":"The slide's first Motown anchors","body":"<p>Berry Gordy, Tamla, and Smokey Robinson's \"Shop Around\" are the core slide anchors. \"Shop Around\" reached #2 on the pop chart. Use those facts as the entry point into the larger Motown sound and crossover argument.</p>"}],"british":[{"type":"learn","tag":"BLUES IN BRITAIN","title":"The British encounter happens before the Invasion","body":"<p>The slide chronology starts with Big Bill Broonzy in 1951, Muddy Waters in 1958, and the American Folk Blues Festival from 1962-66. The London Times reaction to Waters, \"screaming guitar and howling piano,\" captures how forceful electric Chicago blues could sound to British audiences.</p>\n     <p>The Keith Richards quotation makes the influence personal: hearing a Muddy Waters song is one thing, suddenly playing next to Muddy is another.</p>"},{"type":"learn","tag":"SKIFFLE","title":"Lead Belly to Lonnie Donegan to British rock","body":"<p>The slides use \"Rock Island Line\" to connect Lead Belly to Lonnie Donegan's skiffle version. Skiffle gave British teenagers a low-barrier way into American folk, blues, and roots repertory.</p>\n     <p>The deck then points toward Jimmy Page and the Quarry Men. The key is not that skiffle sounds identical to later rock, but that it trains a generation of British youth to form bands around American roots material.</p>"}]},"timeline":[{"year":"1857","title":"Phonautograph","text":"Edouard-Leon Scott de Martinville records visual traces of sound."},{"year":"1877","title":"Edison phonograph","text":"Recorded sound begins separating performance from listening."},{"year":"1895","title":"Berliner disc era","text":"Short disc capacity helps standardize compact recorded forms."},{"year":"1896 / 1901","title":"Marconi wireless milestones","text":"Radio technology moves from patent to transatlantic transmission."},{"year":"1917","title":"Original Dixieland Jass Band","text":"Victor issues the first widely recognized commercial jazz record, making jazz reproducible mass culture."},{"year":"1920","title":"Mamie Smith, Crazy Blues","text":"A million-selling success in the course notes proves a major Black consumer market."},{"year":"1924","title":"WLS National Barn Dance","text":"Radio begins nationalizing old-time/rural music."},{"year":"1925","title":"WSM Barn Dance","text":"The Nashville broadcast that becomes the Grand Ole Opry."},{"year":"1927","title":"Bristol Sessions + Grand Ole Opry","text":"Country recording and broadcasting become major national institutions."},{"year":"1932","title":"Electric guitar on the technology timeline","text":"Amplification will transform blues and rock guitar vocabulary."},{"year":"1936-37","title":"Robert Johnson recordings","text":"Twenty-nine recordings later become central to blues mythology and the rock canon."},{"year":"1939","title":"Opry on NBC","text":"National broadcasting strengthens Nashville's identity as country-music capital."},{"year":"1941","title":"Muddy Waters recorded by Lomax","text":"The rural-to-urban migration story is documented before Chicago electrification."},{"year":"1943-44","title":"Muddy Waters moves and electrifies","text":"Chicago migration followed by the shift from acoustic to electric guitar."},{"year":"1946","title":"Late-night R&B on clear-channel radio","text":"Powerful broadcasts such as WLAC help R&B cross geography and racial markets."},{"year":"1947-49","title":"Transistor, LP, and 45 era","text":"Portability and new formats reshape youth consumption and the single/album market."},{"year":"1949","title":"Chess established","text":"Leonard and Phil Chess build a key Chicago blues and early-rock institution."},{"year":"1950","title":"Memphis Recording Service","text":"Sam Phillips opens a studio to record Southern artists with few commercial outlets."},{"year":"1951","title":"Rocket 88 + rock label emerging","text":"R&B-derived dance music is increasingly described as rock and roll."},{"year":"1954","title":"Elvis at Sun + transistor portability","text":"That's All Right and Blue Moon of Kentucky combine Black blues and white country sources."},{"year":"1955","title":"Maybellene + Blackboard Jungle","text":"Chuck Berry and film culture accelerate the youth-market breakthrough of rock."},{"year":"1956","title":"Elvis on national television","text":"Rock becomes visual spectacle as well as sound, intensifying moral panic."},{"year":"Late 1950s","title":"Covers, teen idols, censorship, payola","text":"The industry and government respond to rock's racial, sexual, and youth-cultural disruption."},{"year":"1959-60","title":"Folk scholarship and reissues","text":"Country Blues and Blues Fell This Morning help frame a revival of traditional music."},{"year":"1962-66","title":"American Folk Blues Festival","text":"British audiences meet major American blues artists directly."},{"year":"Early 1960s","title":"Dylan and the folk revival","text":"Traditional repertory and political songwriting converge."},{"year":"1960s","title":"Wall of Sound + Motown","text":"Producer control and label systems make studio production central to pop identity."},{"year":"1964","title":"British Invasion","text":"British bands recirculate American blues, R&B, rock, and skiffle influences on a global scale."},{"year":"Late 1960s","title":"Counterculture","text":"Rock becomes deeply entangled with politics, social identity, experimentation, and alternative culture."},{"year":"1969","title":"Woodstock","text":"The festival becomes a central symbol in later collective memory of the counterculture."}]};
